# Line-ending policy for aios-system.
#
# Without an explicit policy, git's `core.autocrlf` is
# the only knob and the value differs per machine.
# Windows checkouts want CRLF for PowerShell scripts;
# Go source, Markdown, YAML, JSON, and shell expect LF.
# The policy below makes both work without warning on
# commit and without conversion churn on rebase.

# Go source: LF, no CRLF.
*.go            text eol=lf

# Tests and configuration: LF.
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf

# PowerShell: CRLF. PowerShell on Windows refuses to
# parse LF-only scripts that contain here-strings.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts: LF (used in test runners and CI).
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# Anything else: let `core.autocrlf` decide. The
# explicit entries above cover the file types that
# actually ship in the repo; this catch-all stops
# git from warning on the rest.
*               text=auto
